\section{Adding versus drawing}{


\code{tinyplot_add()} layers new elements \emph{on top} of the existing plot. The
complementary, top-level \verb{tinyplot(..., draw = <>)} argument works in the
opposite direction: it layers \emph{underneath} the main plot elements.

However, note that \code{draw} is fully generic---it accepts any drawing or
annotation expression---and this includes \code{tinyplot_add()} itself. Passing
\code{draw = tinyplot_add(...)} thus yields a regular tinyplot layer, inheriting
from the enclosing call in the usual way, but drawn below the main elements
rather than above them. See Examples.
